#!/usr/bin/env bash
#
# wk session on [--bmc] | gdm [--bmc] | off | status -- a real graphical
# session for runs, or a real desktop, on real hardware.
#
# GPU benchmarks need a real DRM session on real hardware. Speedometer and
# MotionMark under llvmpipe are not slow results, they are a different
# measurement, and a headless run has no display cadence for MotionMark to
# measure at all.
#
# `on` is the benchmark session: a single-client kiosk compositor (cage),
# nothing else running on seat0. Three cases, in the order they are checked:
#
#   Someone is logged in at the machine. Nothing to do -- the compositor is
#   already there; wk uses its socket.
#
#   A wk session is already running. Nothing to do.
#
#   Nobody is logged in. Stop the display manager and start cage on seat0.
#   That is the unattended case, and the only one that needs privilege.
#
# `gdm` is the opposite of `on`: a real, loggable-into desktop instead of a
# benchmark kiosk, for when debugging needs more than one window. `off` stops
# both and leaves the screen dark -- it does not bring anything back, so
# getting a desktop back is `wk session gdm`, not `wk session off` followed by
# waiting.
#
# `off` is two things, and it was broken for as long as it was only one of
# them:
#
#   The outputs are modeset off -- the CRTC disabled, not painted black. A
#   compositor holding an output and drawing black is still scanning out a real
#   mode, so the monitor has a signal and stays lit; the screen was black and
#   the backlight was on, which is what "off doesn't turn off the display"
#   turned out to mean. cage has no DPMS knob of its own, but it does export
#   wlr-output-management, and disabling a head there is a real modeset.
#
#   A placeholder compositor keeps holding the device afterwards. Releasing DRM
#   master and trusting the kernel's own fbcon to blank the now-masterless
#   outputs was the first design, and on this board's NVIDIA driver that trust
#   doesn't pay off -- fb_blank stops the console being redrawn without reaching
#   real hardware, so whatever was on screen stays there, frozen, looking
#   exactly like a working blank failed.
#
# Neither half substitutes for the other: the modeset is what darkens the
# monitor, the placeholder is what stops fbcon lighting it back up. See
# admin/wk-quiesce-priv's `session_on off` and `outputs_off` for the mechanics.
#
# The compositor's Wayland socket lives in a directory of its own
# ($XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/wk/display), not in the user's runtime directory, so that a
# workspace can be handed the socket without being handed the session D-Bus
# socket that sits next to it.
#
# --- what --bmc is for -------------------------------------------------------
# This machine has two DRM devices: the GPU, and the ASPEED BMC's display-only
# `ast` chip whose framebuffer is what the BMC serves over KVM-over-IP. The
# default session names the GPU alone, so the BMC cannot end up in the display
# path of a measured run -- that is what "perf runs disable the BMC" means here,
# and it is enforced by the device list rather than by hoping wlroots enumerates
# the way it did last time.
#
# --bmc is the opposite trade, for debugging a GUI from somewhere else: it moves
# the session to where the BMC can see it. The BMC's chip becomes the only
# modesetting device the compositor has, so the monitor goes dark and the one
# output is the one served over KVM-over-IP. The ast has no render node of its
# own, so this is a software-rendered session -- slower than a real one, and
# never a number to compare with anything.
#
# Rendering on the GPU and letting wlroots copy each finished frame across to
# the ast was tried and dropped: on this board's NVIDIA + ast pairing the copy
# never works, and it doesn't fail loudly -- the compositor comes up, the
# socket appears, and it just never produces a frame the ast connector can
# show, forever. Software rendering is slower still, but it actually shows
# something. The mode is recorded in /run/wk-session-mode, and everything that
# could produce a number from it says so.
#
# --- what gdm mode is for ----------------------------------------------------
# `on` and `on --bmc` are a kiosk: cage, one window, nothing to log into. `gdm`
# starts the actual display manager and desktop instead, for debugging that
# needs more than a single browser window -- a terminal next to it, a second
# app, anything a real session can do that a kiosk can't.
#
# cage gets its one device from WLR_DRM_DEVICES; gdm's compositor (mutter) has
# no equivalent knob, so `gdm --bmc` reaches for the only lever that actually
# constrains it: the GPU is stripped of the udev tags that make it a seat
# member at all (the same tags that removing it would strip), so it isn't on
# seat0 or on any other seat either -- moving it to a different seat instead
# of off every seat was tried first, and gdm promptly started a second
# greeter there, one session per chip instead of one. With the GPU gone from
# every seat, mutter has nothing left to pick but the ast. `gdm` without
# --bmc does the same in reverse, hiding the ast so the desktop can't land on
# the management chip by mistake. `off` clears whichever hide is in effect
# along with everything else -- the seat is never left with a device
# missing.
#
# That lever reaches mutter and nothing else, which took a while to notice
# because it fails silently in the most convincing way available: `gdm --bmc`
# hid the GPU successfully and the desktop appeared on the monitor anyway.
# Ubuntu's 61-gdm.rules sends this machine to `gdm_prefer_xorg` on sight of
# nvidia_drm with modeset=Y -- that branch ends in an unconditional GOTO, so
# every driver version takes it -- and NVIDIA's Xorg driver never opens
# /dev/dri/cardN at all. It drives the card through /dev/nvidia0,
# /dev/nvidiactl and /dev/nvidia-modeset: character devices, mode 0666, with no
# udev properties and no seat tags for TAG-="seat" to remove. The hide was
# real and irrelevant. So both gdm modes now force the greeter onto Wayland
# (gdm's own runtime config, so the override lives in /run) and then check the
# greeter's session type, because a mode that isn't being enforced should say
# so rather than be believed.

set -euo pipefail
WK_ROOT="$(cd "$(dirname "$0")/.." && pwd)"
. "$WK_ROOT/lib/common.sh"

is_linux || die "wk session is Linux-only (macOS workspaces have no GPU to reach)"

PRIV=/usr/local/libexec/wk-quiesce-priv
ACTION="${1:-status}"
shift || true
BMC=""
while [ $# -gt 0 ]; do
    case "$1" in
        --bmc|--mirror) BMC=1; shift ;;
        *) die "usage: wk session on [--bmc] | gdm [--bmc] | off | status" ;;
    esac
done

RUNTIME="${XDG_RUNTIME_DIR:-/run/user/$(id -u)}/wk/display"
SOCKET="$RUNTIME/wayland-0"

# Returns the helper's own exit status, and lets its stderr through. That
# matters here in a way it did not before: `session-on-bmc` is *allowed* to
# fail -- it is an attempt at something the two drivers may refuse -- and a
# wrapper that turned any failure into a password prompt, or into a die, could
# not express that. Whether sudo needs a password is therefore asked once, with
# a read-only verb, instead of being inferred from a verb that did something.
PRIV_SUDO=""
run_priv() {
    [ -x "$PRIV" ] || die "$PRIV is not installed -- run: ./setup --stage quiesce"
    if [ -z "$PRIV_SUDO" ]; then
        if sudo -n "$PRIV" session-status >/dev/null 2>&1; then
            PRIV_SUDO=-n
        else
            warn "passwordless sudo unavailable for the helper; re-run ./setup --stage quiesce"
            PRIV_SUDO=ask
        fi
    fi
    if [ "$PRIV_SUDO" = -n ]; then
        sudo -n "$PRIV" "$1"
    else
        sudo "$PRIV" "$1"
    fi
}

# --- which connector belongs to which chip -----------------------------------
# The privileged helper asks the same question about device nodes; this asks it
# about connector names. Deliberately not shared: the helper is root-owned and
# copied out of this repo at install time, and a root script that sourced a file
# from a user-writable checkout would be the escalation the whole arrangement is
# built to avoid.
#
# The kernel driver is the discriminator, never the card number -- card1 is the
# ast here only because of PCI enumeration order.
drm_driver() {
    local link
    link=$(readlink -f "/sys/class/drm/$1/device/driver" 2>/dev/null) || true
    [ -n "$link" ] && basename "$link" || echo none
}

# Prints "NAME status" per connector, e.g. "DP-2 connected".
# $1: ast for the BMC's connectors, not-ast for everything else.
_connectors() {
    local which="$1" card drv conn name
    for card in /sys/class/drm/card[0-9]*; do
        [ -d "$card" ] || continue
        card=$(basename "$card")
        # /sys/class/drm holds cardN and cardN-CONNECTOR-M side by side; only
        # the former is a device.
        case "$card" in *-*) continue ;; esac
        drv=$(drm_driver "$card")
        case "$which" in
            ast)     [ "$drv" = ast ] || continue ;;
            not-ast) case "$drv" in ast|none) continue ;; esac ;;
        esac
        for conn in "/sys/class/drm/$card"-*; do
            [ -d "$conn" ] || continue
            name=$(basename "$conn")
            printf '%s %s\n' "${name#"$card"-}" "$(cat "$conn/status" 2>/dev/null || echo unknown)"
        done
    done
}

# Connected connectors that are still being driven, by name -- the difference
# between "black" and "off", and the only way to tell them apart without a
# monitor in front of you. Both files are needed: `dpms` reads On for a
# connector whose CRTC was never enabled at all, so `enabled` is what says
# whether anything is being scanned out to it.
#
# $1 selects a chip, same vocabulary as _connectors: all, not-ast, ast. `off`
# asks about not-ast, because the GPU's outputs are the ones it can reach; the
# status line asks about all of them, because overstating that reach is how this
# went unnoticed in the first place.
_lit_connectors() {
    local which="${1:-all}" card drv conn name out=""
    for card in /sys/class/drm/card[0-9]*; do
        [ -d "$card" ] || continue
        card=$(basename "$card")
        case "$card" in *-*) continue ;; esac
        drv=$(drm_driver "$card")
        case "$which" in
            ast)     [ "$drv" = ast ] || continue ;;
            not-ast) case "$drv" in ast|none) continue ;; esac ;;
        esac
        for conn in "/sys/class/drm/$card"-*; do
            [ -d "$conn" ] || continue
            [ "$(cat "$conn/status" 2>/dev/null)" = connected ] || continue
            [ "$(cat "$conn/enabled" 2>/dev/null)" = enabled ] || continue
            [ "$(cat "$conn/dpms" 2>/dev/null)" = On ] || continue
            name=$(basename "$conn")
            out="${out:+$out }${name#"$card"-}"
        done
    done
    printf '%s' "$out"
}

# The outputs the running compositor actually took, by connector name. This is
# the only way to know: wlroots is free to drop a device it cannot render for,
# and it does so without a word.
session_outputs() {
    have wayland-info || return 0
    { WAYLAND_DISPLAY="$SOCKET" wayland-info 2>/dev/null || true; } \
        | sed -n 's/^[[:space:]]*name: \([A-Za-z][A-Za-z0-9]*-[0-9][0-9]*\)$/\1/p'
}

session_display_mode() {
    have wayland-info || return 0
    { WAYLAND_DISPLAY="$SOCKET" wayland-info 2>/dev/null || true; } \
        | sed -n 's/.*width: \([0-9]*\) px, height: \([0-9]*\) px, refresh: \([0-9.]*\) Hz.*/\1x\2 @ \3Hz/p' \
        | head -1
}

# The display manager greeter's session type on seat0, or nothing if there isn't
# one yet. This is the fact that decides whether `gdm --bmc` means anything: see
# the note on gdm modes above.
# $1: how many quarter-seconds to keep looking. Starting gdm wants to wait for
# the greeter to appear; `status` wants today's answer and no pause at all.
_greeter_type() {
    have loginctl || return 1
    local i sid class typ
    for i in $(seq 1 "${1:-40}"); do
        for sid in $(loginctl list-sessions --no-legend 2>/dev/null | awk '$4 == "seat0" {print $1}'); do
            class=$(loginctl show-session "$sid" -p Class --value 2>/dev/null || echo)
            [ "$class" = greeter ] || continue
            typ=$(loginctl show-session "$sid" -p Type --value 2>/dev/null || echo)
            [ -n "$typ" ] && { printf '%s' "$typ"; return 0; }
        done
        sleep 0.25
    done
    return 1
}

# Our own compositor's logind session leader, or nothing if it isn't running.
#
# This is the only thing that tells our session apart from a person's. cage gets
# its seat from PAMName=login, which is not a detail we can drop -- without it
# libseat cannot take DRM master and the compositor exits on the spot -- and the
# session it produces is Class=user, Type=wayland, on seat0: a logged-in desktop
# in every field logind exposes. What differs is which process leads it, and
# because the unit's ExecStart `exec`s cage, the unit's MainPID *is* that leader.
wk_session_leader() {
    local pid
    pid=$(systemctl show wk-session -p MainPID --value 2>/dev/null || true)
    case "$pid" in ''|0) return 1 ;; esac
    printf '%s' "$pid"
}

# A graphical session belonging to a logged-in *person*, which we neither own
# nor should disturb.
#
# Class matters as much as Type here. The display manager's greeter is itself a
# graphical session on seat0 -- gdm's shows up as Type=x11, Class=greeter, owned
# by the gdm user -- and treating it as somebody's desktop means `wk session on`
# politely declines to do the one thing it exists for, on exactly the machine
# state it was written for: nobody logged in, a greeter waiting.
#
# And our own compositor has to be excluded by name, because no field on it says
# "not a person" -- see wk_session_leader. Leaving it in is not a cosmetic bug:
# `wk session off` leaves a placeholder compositor running by design, so every
# later `wk session gdm` found a "foreign desktop", declined, and advised
# running `wk session off` first -- the one command guaranteed to put the
# session it was complaining about back.
foreign_session() {
    have loginctl || return 1
    local sid typ class leader ours=""
    ours=$(wk_session_leader) || ours=""
    for sid in $(loginctl list-sessions --no-legend 2>/dev/null | awk '$0 ~ /seat0/ {print $1}'); do
        typ=$(loginctl show-session "$sid" -p Type --value 2>/dev/null || echo)
        class=$(loginctl show-session "$sid" -p Class --value 2>/dev/null || echo)
        [ "$class" = user ] || continue
        if [ -n "$ours" ]; then
            leader=$(loginctl show-session "$sid" -p Leader --value 2>/dev/null || echo)
            [ "$leader" = "$ours" ] && continue
        fi
        case "$typ" in
            wayland|x11) echo "$sid $typ"; return 0 ;;
        esac
    done
    return 1
}

case "$ACTION" in
on)
    # The socket file outliving the compositor is the normal case after a
    # crash or an unclean stop, and treating its presence as "already running"
    # means `wk session on` silently does nothing on exactly the machine that
    # needs it most. The unit's state is the fact; the socket is a hint.
    if [ -S "$SOCKET" ] && systemctl is-active --quiet wk-session 2>/dev/null; then
        # ... unless what is running is the other kind of session. Asking for a
        # benchmark session and getting "already running" for a software one is
        # how a whole afternoon of numbers turns out to have measured llvmpipe.
        wanted=gpu; [ -n "$BMC" ] && wanted=bmc
        running=$(session_mode)
        if [ "$wanted" = "$running" ]; then
            info "wk session already running ($SOCKET, mode $running)"
            session_mode_warn || true
            exit 0
        fi
        info "a '$running' session is running; restarting it as '$wanted'"
        run_priv session-stop
    elif [ -S "$SOCKET" ]; then
        warn "stale compositor socket at $SOCKET -- starting a new session"
    fi

    if session=$(foreign_session); then
        info "a graphical session is already active on seat0 (session $session)"
        log  "  leaving it alone; run 'wk session off' first if you want a clean"
        log  "  benchmark compositor instead of a full desktop"
        exit 0
    fi

    if [ -z "$BMC" ]; then
        info "starting the benchmark compositor on seat0 (GPU only, BMC excluded)"
        run_priv session-on || die "the compositor did not start; journalctl -u wk-session"
        [ -S "$SOCKET" ] || die "no Wayland socket at $SOCKET after start"
    else
        # The session moves to the BMC's output, it is not duplicated onto it:
        # the BMC's chip becomes the compositor's only modesetting device, so
        # there is exactly one output and no question about which one the window
        # is on. The monitor goes dark for the duration.
        log "  the monitor goes dark: the session moves to the BMC, it is not duplicated"
        info "starting a BMC session on seat0 (software-rendered)"
        run_priv session-on-bmc || die "the compositor did not start; journalctl -u wk-session"
        [ -S "$SOCKET" ] || die "no Wayland socket at $SOCKET after start"

        # The compositor coming up is not by itself proof that the output it took
        # is the BMC's, and a blank remote console is the one failure this mode
        # cannot afford to report as success.
        outs=$(session_outputs)
        bmc_names=$(_connectors ast | awk '{print $1}')
        found=""
        for name in $bmc_names; do
            case " $(echo "$outs" | tr '\n' ' ') " in *" $name "*) found=1 ;; esac
        done
        [ -n "$found" ] || warn "the session is up but not on a BMC connector (outputs: ${outs:-none})"
    fi

    info "session up: $SOCKET (mode $(session_mode))"
    log  "  outputs: $(session_outputs | tr '\n' ' ')  display: $(session_display_mode)"
    log  "  workspaces see it at /run/wk/display/wayland-0"
    log  "  launch a browser in it with:  wk gui <workspace> [url]"
    [ -n "$BMC" ] && log  "  watch it on the BMC's KVM-over-IP console" || true
    session_mode_warn || true
    ;;

gdm)
    if session=$(foreign_session); then
        info "a graphical session is already active on seat0 (session $session)"
        log  "  leaving it alone; run 'wk session off' first if you want a clean start"
        exit 0
    fi

    if systemctl is-active --quiet wk-session 2>/dev/null; then
        info "stopping the benchmark compositor to start a desktop instead"
    fi

    if [ -z "$BMC" ]; then
        info "starting gdm on seat0 (GPU only, the BMC's chip hidden from seat0)"
        run_priv session-gdm || die "gdm did not start; journalctl -u gdm"
    else
        log "  the monitor goes dark: the GPU is hidden from seat0, not just idle"
        info "starting gdm on seat0 (BMC only, software-rendered)"
        run_priv session-gdm-bmc || die "gdm did not start; journalctl -u gdm"
    fi

    # Which chip the desktop lands on is only enforced if the greeter is a
    # compositor that takes its devices from logind. On Xorg it is not enforced
    # at all -- NVIDIA's DDX drives the card through /dev/nvidia*, which carries
    # no seat tags to strip -- so the type is checked rather than assumed.
    greeter=$(_greeter_type) || greeter=""
    case "$greeter" in
        wayland)
            info "gdm is up on seat0 (wayland greeter) -- log in at the console"
            ;;
        "")
            warn "gdm was started but no greeter session appeared on seat0"
            log  "  journalctl -u gdm"
            ;;
        *)
            warn "the greeter came up on $greeter, not wayland -- the mode is not enforced"
            log  "  hiding a device from seat0 constrains a compositor that asks logind for"
            log  "  its devices. NVIDIA's Xorg driver doesn't: it drives the card through"
            log  "  /dev/nvidia0 and /dev/nvidia-modeset, character devices with no udev"
            log  "  properties and no seat tags for the hide to remove. So the desktop is"
            log  "  wherever that driver put it, whichever mode was asked for."
            log  "  gdm chose Xorg via 61-gdm.rules (nvidia_drm + modeset=Y falls through"
            log  "  to gdm_prefer_xorg); wk overrides it, so this means the override"
            log  "  didn't take -- check: sudo $PRIV session-status"
            ;;
    esac
    [ -n "$BMC" ] && log "  or over the BMC's KVM-over-IP console" || true
    ;;

off)
    run_priv session-off || die "the helper failed to turn the screen off"

    # Two separate claims, easily conflated: the placeholder compositor is
    # running (so fbcon can't repaint the console over the top), *and* its
    # outputs have been modeset off (so the monitor has no signal and sleeps).
    # The second is checked from sysfs rather than announced.
    lit=$(_lit_connectors not-ast)
    if [ -n "$lit" ]; then
        warn "the screen is black but still lit: $lit"
        log  "  a compositor holding an output and painting it black is not an"
        log  "  output that is off -- the CRTC keeps scanning out, so the monitor"
        log  "  keeps its signal. The modeset that darkens it needs wlr-randr:"
        log  "    ./setup --stage tools   (then: wk session off)"
    else
        info "screen off -- outputs modeset off, placeholder compositor holding the seat"
        log  "  both halves are load-bearing: disabling the outputs is what darkens"
        log  "  the monitor, holding the device is what stops fbcon repainting the"
        log  "  console over the top of it"
    fi

    # The placeholder covers one DRM device (see the helper's `off` mode), so the
    # BMC's chip keeps whatever was last drawn on it. Said out loud rather than
    # left for someone to find on a KVM console.
    bmc_lit=$(_lit_connectors ast)
    [ -n "$bmc_lit" ] && log "  still lit on the BMC's chip: $bmc_lit (its console keeps its last frame)"
    log  "  a desktop back:  wk session gdm      a benchmark session:  wk session on"
    ;;

status)
    # Everything here is readable without privilege -- unit state, the socket,
    # the display manager, the compositor's mode -- so status does not go
    # through the helper at all: a failure inside the helper would then mean no
    # status output whatsoever, which is the opposite of what a status command
    # is for.
    unit=$(systemctl is-active wk-session 2>/dev/null | head -1 || true)
    dm=$(systemctl is-active gdm3 2>/dev/null | head -1 || true)
    [ -n "$dm" ] || dm=$(systemctl is-active gdm 2>/dev/null | head -1 || true)

    printf 'session:   %s\n' "${unit:-unknown}"
    printf 'socket:    %s\n' "$([ -S "$SOCKET" ] && echo "$SOCKET" || echo none)"
    printf 'dm:        %s\n' "${dm:-unknown}"

    # A few root-only facts, and not worth a password for any of them: nvidia-drm
    # without modesetting has no KMS for a compositor to drive; which device (if
    # any) is hidden from seat0 is invisible from anywhere else.
    priv_status=$(sudo -n "$PRIV" session-status 2>/dev/null || true)
    modeset=$(printf '%s\n' "$priv_status" | awk '/^modeset:/ {print $2}')
    desktop=$(printf '%s\n' "$priv_status" | awk '/^desktop:/ {print $2}')
    seathide=$(printf '%s\n' "$priv_status" | awk '/^seat-hide:/ {print $2}')
    printf 'modeset:   %s\n' "${modeset:-unknown}"

    # Which chip the session is on, and therefore whether anything measured
    # against it can be believed.
    printf 'mode:      %s\n' "$(session_mode)"
    # Which connected outputs are actually lit. In `off` mode this must be empty;
    # anything listed here while the mode says off is the bug this line exists to
    # make visible from a shell instead of from a chair in front of the monitor.
    lit=$(_lit_connectors)
    printf 'lit:       %s\n' "${lit:-none}"
    printf 'gpu:       %s\n' "$(_connectors not-ast | tr '\n' ' ' | sed 's/ *$//')"
    printf 'bmc:       %s\n' "$(_connectors ast | tr '\n' ' ' | sed 's/ *$//')"
    printf 'desktop:   %s\n' "${desktop:-none}"

    # A desktop mode is only enforced when the greeter is a compositor that takes
    # its devices from logind, which on this machine means Wayland. `x11` here
    # means `desktop:` above is intent rather than fact.
    greeter=$(_greeter_type 1) || greeter=""
    [ -n "$greeter" ] && printf 'greeter:   %s%s\n' "$greeter" \
        "$([ "$greeter" = wayland ] || echo '  (mode NOT enforced -- see gdm notes)')"
    [ -n "$seathide" ] && printf 'seat-hide: %s (hidden from seat0)\n' "$seathide"

    if [ -S "$SOCKET" ]; then
        printf 'outputs:   %s\n' "$(session_outputs | tr '\n' ' ')"
    fi

    # The display mode matters: a fullscreen browser's surface is the display
    # mode, and MotionMark scores scale with surface size -- so two runs on
    # different modes are not comparable. cage takes the connector's preferred
    # mode, which is not always the largest one it supports.
    if [ -S "$SOCKET" ]; then
        mode=$(session_display_mode)
        [ -n "$mode" ] && printf 'display:   %s\n' "$mode"
    fi

    # Our own compositor is a logind session too -- PAMName=login is what gives
    # it the seat -- so it matches foreign_session exactly. If our socket is
    # there, the session on seat0 is ours and calling it foreign is just wrong.
    if [ ! -S "$SOCKET" ] && session=$(foreign_session); then
        printf 'foreign:   session %s\n' "$session"
    fi
    session_mode_warn || true
    exit 0
    ;;

*)
    die "usage: wk session on [--bmc] | gdm [--bmc] | off | status"
    ;;
esac
